//? Title: Square-root digits: actual exact prefixes and remainders
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An unsigned integer square root needs a decimal expansion with a known
truncation direction. The classical paired-digit extraction chooses the greatest
next digit whose square increment fits the remainder. We inspect actual exact
integer states, retain the original roots and thirteen-digit accuracy
experiment, and remove fixed-width prefix overflow.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

Group the radicand into decimal pairs, padding a leading zero when needed. Bring
each pair down by multiplying the remainder by100; after the integer pairs,
bring down zero pairs for fractional digits. If R is the current root prefix,
the next candidate digit d changes its square by

$$(10R+d)^2-100R^2=(20R+d)d.$$
\alttext(Appending decimal digit d changes the scaled squared root prefix by twenty times R plus d, multiplied by d.)

Choose the largest fitting digit0..9, subtract this exact increment and append
it. Induction preserves nonnegative remainder and gives the greatest prefix
whose square does not exceed the brought-down radicand. After f fractional
digits, the point-free output is floor(sqrt(n)*10^f). Thus

$$0\leq\sqrt n-r_f<10^{-f}.$$
\alttext(The exact truncated decimal root never exceeds the true root and its error is less than one unit in the last requested decimal place.)

The error bound improves by a factor ten per digit; the observed error need not
shrink by exactly that factor at every step. Arbitrary-precision root, product
and remainder values avoid historical uint64 overflow. At most ten candidate
comparisons are made per emitted digit, but their integer bit cost grows with
the prefix. Displayed double comparisons are approximate references, not exact
correctness certificates.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/sqrt_digits_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/random/quality.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/sqrt_digits.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = a::sqrt_digits_trace(152, 2);
    std::print("| actual step | pair brought down | selected digit | actual integer root prefix | exact " "remainder |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | --- |\n");
    for (const auto &s : r.history)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | `{}` | `{}` |\n", s.step, s.pair, s.digit,
                   s.prefix, s.remainder);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Radicand152, pairs01 then52");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} actual candidate tests",
                                     r.candidate_tests));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Exact truncated root {}", r.digits));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/sqrt_digits_tiny.svg");
}
```
